=== 7. THE DESCRIPTIONS ARE TRUE OF THE CODE AS IT STANDS -- PASS ===
Read against behavior, from the description string the CLIENT actually receives
(ListTools over a live session), not from the source literal. Claim by claim,
each checked against output quoted above:

search_transactions description:
 a) "type (income, outflow, or transfer; \"expense\" is also accepted as an
    alias for outflow; omit for all three)" -- TRUE: all four values drive
    successfully, omit returns all three types (total=5, all three types in the
    page). The `type` field's own jsonschema string matches, and I read it back
    off the wire: "income, outflow, or transfer (expense is accepted as an alias
    for outflow); omit for all three".
 b) "sum_amount is the signed sum over ALL matches, not just this page" -- TRUE:
    per_page=2 still reports sum_amount 3850.
 c) "on the default path (no type filter) it EXCLUDES Transfer rows even though
    they are still listed (`total` and the page include them)" -- TRUE in both
    halves, item 3. This is the sentence that would have been the inverse of the
    code had the fix dropped the rows; it is not.
 d) "netting it into a signed total would make this sum disagree with
    summarize_spending's net_savings for the same window" -- TRUE: the
    disagreement is exactly what attempt 1 measured (800 vs 3800) and exactly
    what is now gone (3850 vs 3850, and 3800 vs 3800 end-to-end).
 e) "with an explicit type filter the filtered set is already one type, so the
    exclusion only matters on the default path" -- TRUE: type=transfer yields
    sum_amount -2300, i.e. the transfers ARE summed when explicitly requested.
 f) the pre-existing closing claim "sums here agree with those tools for the same
    window (the dashboard, get_anomalies, get_price_creep, summarize_spending)"
    -- was FALSE at attempt 1 and is TRUE now on the default path.

The implementation cannot drift from (c)/(e): the switch keys on
strings.ToLower(in.Type) with `case ""` (search.go:150) and the sum branches on
`in.Type == ""` (search.go:174). ToLower(x)=="" iff x=="", so the two conditions
are the same condition -- there is no argument (whitespace included: " " falls to
default and errors) that takes the no-filter sum branch while a filter was
applied, or vice versa.

server.go:
 g) "is a TRANSFER: the third transaction type (Transfer), alongside Income and
    Outflow" -- TRUE (models/transaction.go:26).
 h) "excluded from both totals by type filter" -- TRUE and now literal:
    summarize_spending reports total_income 5000 / total_expenses 1200 with the
    -3000 transfer in the ledger, and search.go:175-176 excludes via
    FilterByType(Income)+FilterByType(Outflow).
 i) "unlike the old drop-on-load filter the rows REMAIN in the ledger and are
    visible through get_transfers" -- TRUE, driven: get_transfers returns them.
 j) "The spending tools therefore do NOT COUNT savings or investment
    contributions in their totals" (attempt 2 tightened this from "do NOT show",
    which was the false half -- the rows ARE shown) -- TRUE of every default
