=== 9. Confirm-token behavior, re-driven -- PASS ===
Not inherited from attempt 1. I rebuilt the filesystem-fingerprint driver
(sha256 over path+content of every regular file under the data dir, sorted) so
any create/delete/byte-change anywhere shows up, each case carrying a
valid-token CONTROL so "nothing changed" cannot pass vacuously.

set_balance_anchor, against a seeded accounts.json (baseline fingerprint
bfbdaf221032, 1 file):
  (a) no token                     -> confirmed=false, token minted, unchanged
  (b) garbage token                -> IsError, unchanged
  (c) token replayed with amount 9999 -> IsError, unchanged
  (d) token replayed for account "savings" -> IsError, unchanged
  (e) token minted for resolve_transfer, redeemed here -> IsError, unchanged
  (f) CONTROL, valid token -> confirmed=true, fingerprint bfbdaf221032 ->
      5f5f8802475d, 1 file -> 2, and accounts.json on disk gained
      {"date":"2026-08-15T00:00:00Z","amount":4210.55} in date order after the
      pre-existing 2026-07-31 anchor
  (g) replay of the now-spent token -> IsError, fingerprint unchanged
Every rejection returned the one ErrBadToken sentence ("confirmation token is not
valid for this call; call the tool again without a token to get a fresh preview
and token"), so forged/wrong-args/wrong-tool/spent are indistinguishable to a
caller -- deliberate, per confirm.go.

resolve_transfer, against a real suspected pair (pair_key f2dd5ca97cb3, two
cross-account equal-amount CSV rows). I asserted on transfer_decisions.json
itself, not on the in-memory queue, so a sidecar written during a failed call
could not hide:
  (a) no token   -> preview, transfer_decisions.json = <absent>
  (b) garbage    -> IsError, still <absent>
  (c) a confirm-token replayed as verdict="reject" -> IsError, still <absent>
      (the dangerous swap: the verdict the user approved cannot be changed)
  (d) token minted for set_balance_anchor -> IsError, still <absent>
  (e) CONTROL, valid token -> confirmed=true and the file appears:
      {"decisions":{"f2dd5ca97cb3":{"pair_key":"f2dd5ca97cb3","stable_ids":
        ["brokerage|2026-08-10|77777|0","checking|2026-08-10|-77777|0"],
        "verdict":"confirm","decided_at":"..."}}}
No refusal path wrote anything, on either tool.

=== 10. Token discipline -- PASS ===
One shared confirm.Registry, not a parallel mechanism: server.go:281-282 passes
`Confirm: adminDeps.Confirm` and `Approvals: adminDeps.Approvals` into
ledger.Deps, which is what makes the cross-tool rejections in 9(e) and 9(d) real
rather than an artifact of separate registries. Check-then-ask-then-Redeem is
used as admin/ does (Check at anchor.go:115 / resolve.go:116; Redeem only inside
applyAnchor anchor.go:186 and applyResolveTransfer resolve.go:194, which are the
only writers and both return before ensureSnapshot on a bad token).

=== 11. No-anchor reports unavailable, never zero -- PASS (re-driven) ===
Attempt-1's fixture (twoAccounts) carries anchors, so I seeded an account with
NO anchors explicitly:
  get_accounts           -> {"count":1,"accounts":[{"id":"checking",
     "available":false,"balance":0,"low_balance":false,"threshold":500}]}
  get_balance_projection -> {"account_id":"checking","available":false,
     "has_reference":false,"threshold":500}   (no crossing, no suggested_top_up)
CONTROL, after recording an anchor of 5000 through set_balance_anchor:
  get_accounts           -> "available":true,"balance":5000,"anchor_date":"2026-08-01"
  get_balance_projection -> "available":true,"minimum":5000,"as_of":"2026-08-18"
So available=false is a live signal, not a stuck field, and low_balance stays
false when the balance is unknown (an unknown balance is not "low").

